hey this is preshtalwalker  imagine you want a can that can hold a  fixed volume v  you could design the can to be wide and  short  in other words it would have a large  radius r  and a small height h  you could also contain the same volume v  in another can which has a little  smaller value for its radius  but it would be a little taller to  contain the same volume v  you could also design a can which is  narrower but it would have to be taller  the question is out of all of these  possible designs  which one has the minimum surface area  the question is  what is the best ratio of the height to  the radius which uses the least material  to contain a fixed volume v  give this problem a try and when you're  ready keep watching the video for the  solution  in any cylinder  the volume is given by the formula  v equals pi r squared h  if we have a fixed volume v  we can solve this equation for the  height so that we get the height in  terms of the radius  the surface area of a cylinder is given  by the area of three different surfaces  there is a top and bottom to the can  which are made up of circles with radius  r  and then there's a side to the can which  we can roll out to be a rectangle  the rectangle has a height h and its  other dimension is equal to the  circumference of the circle  we can add up the areas of these shapes  to get the surface area of the cylinder  we can now substitute in our expression  for the height in terms of the volume  and the radius  we then simplify this equation to get  the surface area in terms of one  variable which is the radius of the can  in order to find the minimum value of  the surface area  we take the derivative with respect to r  and set it equal to zero  this gives us four pi r minus two v  divided by r squared equals zero  we'll solve this equation for the  variable v  to get two pi r cubed  now just to check  we'll do the second derivative and make  sure that it's positive so that we know  this is a minimum  we now have two expressions for the  volume  we have one expression for the volume  where we have the minimum surface area  we have another expression for the  volume which holds true for all  cylinders  we can set these equal to each other  we can now simplify this  to get that the only way this is  possible is if the variable h is equal  to 2 times r  in other words we need our height to be  equal to two times the radius when we  have the minimum surface area  and this gives us the optimal ratio of  the height to the radius is equal to two  so this is the can size which uses the  least amount of material to contain the  fixed volume v  did you figure this out  thanks for watching this video please  subscribe to my channel i make videos on  math and game theory you can catch me on  my blog mind your decisions which you  can follow on facebook google plus and  patreon you can catch me on social media  at preshellwalker  if you like this video please check out  my books there are links in the video  description

